L["transfiguration"] = """About a week after Peter said that Jesus was the Messiah, Jesus took three of his closest disciples, Peter, James and John, and led them up a high mountain by themselves. Just before this, Jesus had told his disciples that he would suffer and be killed and then rise again. They did not understand, and Peter had argued with him. Now Jesus gave them a glimpse of who he really was.

There he was transfigured before them. His face shone like the sun, and his clothes became as white as the light. Mark says they were whiter than anyone in the world could bleach them. Then Moses and Elijah appeared, talking with Jesus. Moses stood for the law, and Elijah for the prophets. Luke tells us they spoke about his departure, which he was about to bring to fulfillment at Jerusalem.

Peter, as usual, spoke first. Lord, it is good for us to be here. If you wish, I will put up three shelters, one for you, one for Moses and one for Elijah. Mark says he did not know what to say, they were so frightened. He wanted to stay on the mountain and hold on to the moment.

While he was still speaking, a bright cloud covered them, and a voice from the cloud said, this is my Son, whom I love. With him I am well pleased. Listen to him! When the disciples heard this, they fell facedown to the ground, terrified. The cloud was the same sign of God's presence that Israel had seen at Sinai.

But Jesus came and touched them. Get up, he said. Don't be afraid. When they looked up, they saw no one except Jesus. Moses and Elijah were gone. Only Jesus remained.

As they were coming down the mountain, Jesus gave them orders. Don't tell anyone what you have seen, until the Son of Man has been raised from the dead. They kept it to themselves.

Why does this matter? The voice from heaven had a particular message. Listen to him. Moses and Elijah were great, but they were pointing to Jesus. And the disciples needed this glimpse of glory before they faced the cross. When Jesus was arrested and killed, they could remember what they had seen on the mountain.

What happened after? At the bottom of the mountain, they met a crowd and a father with a boy that the other disciples had not been able to help. Jesus healed him. Real life was waiting below. And Peter never forgot it. Years later he wrote, we did not follow cleverly devised stories when we told you about the coming of our Lord Jesus Christ in power, but we were eyewitnesses of his majesty. We ourselves heard this voice that came from heaven when we were with him on the sacred mountain.

What does this mean for us today? Peter wanted to stay on the mountain, and many of us would too. Mountaintop moments are wonderful, but they are not where we live. Jesus led his disciples back down to the ordinary and the hard. The glimpse of glory was meant to carry them through the valley. And the Father's instruction is for us as well. Listen to him. In the middle of a lot of voices, it is Jesus we should listen to most.

That is why this story is so important. It shows that Jesus is the Son of God in glory, greater than Moses and Elijah. And it gives us a promise that the one who walks with us through the valley is the same one who shines on the mountain."""
